## Overview

**FriESP** is an embedded systems project that turns an ESP32 microcontroller into a small electronic character with animated facial expressions. The character is designed to react to its environment: temperature changes alter its expression (feeling hot or cold), and physical movement or rotation makes it appear dizzy.

The project combines three areas of embedded development:

- **Graphics rendering** on a small TFT display.
- **Sensor integration** (temperature and motion).
- **Interactive behavior** driven by user input and environmental data.

Development is incremental and validated on physical hardware at every stage.

---

## Features

| Feature | Description | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Display initialization | Low-level ST7789 driver with configurable orientation and color inversion | Done |
| Scrolling text banner | Pac-Man style wraparound scrolling with a title and subtitle | Done |
| Screen rotation | Four orientations (landscape, portrait, and both inverted) switched by a button | Done |
| Color themes | Four background/foreground pairs switched by a button | Done |
| Animated face | Eyes, mouth, and basic expressions | In progress |
| Blinking and facial movement | Dynamic idle animations | Planned |
| Temperature-based expressions | Hot and cold reactions | Planned |
| Motion and rotation detection | Dizziness simulation | Planned |
| Automatic expression changes | Behavior engine combining all inputs | Planned |

