## Hardware

### Current development board

| Component | Specification |
|---|---|
| Board | LilyGO TTGO T-Display |
| Microcontroller | ESP32 (dual-core Xtensa LX6, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth) |
| Display | 1.14" IPS TFT, 240 x 135 pixels, ST7789 controller, RGB565 |
| Buttons | 2 onboard (GPIO 0 and GPIO 35) |
| Connection | USB-C |

### Optional sensors (external modules)

The T-Display does **not** include motion or temperature sensors. To implement the reactive features, external modules connected over I2C are required:

| Function | Suggested module | Interface |
|---|---|---|
| Accelerometer and gyroscope | MPU6050 or MPU9250 | I2C |
| Temperature (and pressure) | BMP280, BME280, or similar | I2C |

> **Note:** The sensor choice is subject to hardware verification. The code is being designed so that the sensor driver can be replaced without changing the rest of the application.

### Porting note

The project originally targeted the ESP32-S3. The ESP32-S3 variant of the T-Display (T-Display S3) uses an 8-bit parallel display bus instead of SPI, so display pins and initialization differ. The current code targets the **classic ESP32** T-Display with an SPI display bus.

---

## Pin Mapping

### Display (ST7789, SPI)

| Signal | GPIO |
|---|---|
| SCK (clock) | 18 |
| MOSI (data) | 19 |
| CS (chip select) | 5 |
| DC (data/command) | 16 |
| RST (reset) | 23 |
| BL (backlight) | 4 |

### User input

| Button | GPIO | Function |
|---|---|---|
| BOOT | 0 | Change color theme |
| Right button | 35 | Rotate screen |

> GPIO 35 is input-only and relies on the board's external pull-up. GPIO 0 uses the internal pull-up. **Do not hold the BOOT button while powering on the board**, as this enters firmware flashing mode.

---

## Software and Technologies

- **Language:** MicroPython
- **Firmware:** MicroPython for ESP32 (`ESP32_GENERIC` build recommended)
- **Display driver:** custom ST7789 routines using `machine.SoftSPI`
- **Graphics:** `framebuf` (RGB565 frame buffers)
- **Development tool:** Thonny IDE

---

## Getting Started

### 1. Flash the firmware

1. Download the **ESP32_GENERIC** build from [micropython.org/download](https://micropython.org/download/ESP32_GENERIC/).
2. In Thonny, open *Tools > Options > Interpreter* and select **MicroPython (ESP32)**.
3. Click *Install or update MicroPython* and follow the prompts.

### 2. Upload the project

1. Clone or download this repository.
2. Open *View > Files* in Thonny.
3. Copy the project files to the root of the device storage.

### 3. Run automatically on boot

MicroPython executes `main.py` at startup. Make sure the entry point is saved on the device with exactly that name, in the root directory.

To check for startup errors, open the Thonny shell and press **Ctrl+D** to soft-reboot the board.

---

## Project Structure

The codebase is organized in modules for maintainability. The planned layout is:

```
FriESP/
├── main.py          # Entry point: main loop and state handling
├── config.py        # Pin mapping, colors, and tunable parameters
├── display.py       # ST7789 driver: initialization, windowing, rotation
├── face.py          # Face rendering: eyes, mouth, expressions, blinking
├── sensors.py       # Temperature and motion sensor abstraction
├── behavior.py      # Logic that maps sensor data to expressions
└── README.md
```

> The current working prototype (display driver, scrolling banner, rotation and color buttons) is contained in a single script and will be split into these modules as development progresses.

---

## Display Notes and Known Issues

- **Hardware SPI vs. SoftSPI.** During bring-up, the display remained blank (backlight on, no image) when using hardware SPI with the installed firmware build. Switching to `SoftSPI` resolved the issue, so the project uses software SPI. This is slower; rendering is therefore optimized by updating only the regions that change.
- **Display offset.** The ST7789 RAM is 240 x 320, while the physical panel is 135 x 240. A per-orientation offset is applied to the address window:

  | Orientation | MADCTL | X offset | Y offset |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | Landscape | `0x60` | 40 | 53 |
  | Portrait | `0xC0` | 53 | 40 |
  | Landscape (inverted) | `0xA0` | 40 | 52 |
  | Portrait (inverted) | `0x00` | 52 | 40 |

- **Color inversion.** This panel requires the inversion command (`0x21`) for correct colors.
- **Byte order.** `framebuf` stores RGB565 in little-endian order, while the display expects big-endian; colors are byte-swapped before drawing.
- **Memory.** Large frame buffers can raise `MemoryError` on the ESP32 without PSRAM. Keep text scales moderate.
- **Automatic rotation.** The T-Display has no motion sensor, so rotation is currently manual (button). Automatic rotation will be possible once an external accelerometer is integrated.

